mido: dump the address space around the launchd fault, and stop the walk lying

0131 named the fault (transl0) and printed the walk, and the two disagreed in a
way that cannot both be true: vm_fault() returned KERN_INVALID_ADDRESS, yet the
walk reported a leaf pte with af=1.  The walk is the liar.  It computed indices
from an address of 0x201e1d34620, which is far outside the pmap it was walking
(max 0x8000000000), so it read whatever descriptor happened to sit at the
truncated index and printed it as if it described the fault.  Skip the walk
when the address is outside [min, max) and say so.

The instruction word is the one hard fact from the last boot:

	insn 0xb9000149          str w9, [x10]
	addr 0x201e1d34620
	x86_64 launchd __TEXT is 0x100000000-0x100068000, and 0xb9000149
	appears nowhere in it

so before guessing where the instruction lives, ask the map.  Print every entry
of the faulting task's map (capped at twenty), then say which entry holds pc and
whether the low 32 bits of the faulting address are mapped.  Those three answers
separate the remaining hypotheses:

  pc in an entry under 0x180000000   the store came from a real image, and the
                                     offset inside it names the function
  low32 mapped                       the pointer was assembled from the wrong
                                     halves when it was read as 64 bits
  low32 also unmapped                the pointer is simply garbage

Also print x0..x15: the register that fed x10 is very likely still live in
x0..x9, and a register dump costs four lines.

Banner becomes "MIDO BUILD 0132 pmap-guard=af10 map-dump".
